  <p>For families in the Ozarks, the boundary between daily life and intentional education often blurs. Between morning chores, managing pasture rotations, repairing equipment, and preserving seasonal harvests, days are full of productive work.</p>
  
  <p>Yet, when homeschooling high school approaches, many parents hit a wall of anxiety: <em>How does this translate to an official high school transcript? How do we meet Missouri’s strict hourly requirements without buying costly curricula or spending six hours chained to a desk?</em></p>
  
  <p>The truth is, <strong>Missouri homeschool law (RSMo § 167.012) is built on flexibility</strong>. Real-world homestead skills qualify as legitimate academic coursework—provided you document them properly.</p>

  <!--Section 1: Requirement Breakdown-->
  <div style="background-color: #f1f8e9; border-left: 5px solid rgb(129, 199, 132); border-radius: 4px; margin: 30px 0px; padding: 20px;">
    <h2 style="color: #2e7d32; font-size: 1.8em; margin-top: 0px;">1. Decoding Missouri’s 1,000-Hour Requirement</h2>
    <p>Missouri statute outlines a straightforward formula for homeschool compliance:</p>
    <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding-left: 10px;">
      <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;">☀️ <strong style="color: #1b5e20;">1,000 Total Hours:</strong> Required per school year (running July 1 through June 30).</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;">📚 <strong style="color: #1b5e20;">600 Core Hours:</strong> Must be in reading, language arts, mathematics, social studies, and science.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;">🏠 <strong style="color: #1b5e20;">400 Home Hours:</strong> At least 400 of the 600 core hours must occur at the primary home location.</li>
      <li>🚜 <strong style="color: #1b5e20;">400 Non-Core / Elective Hours:</strong> Can cover physical education, practical arts, vocational trades, agricultural mechanics, and community-based field trips.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <p>Across a standard 180-day school year, 1,000 hours breaks down to roughly <strong>5.5 hours per day</strong>. If your student is active on the homestead, a significant portion of those hours is already happening naturally.</p>

